	doi = {10.1016/j.envsoft.2016.02.022},
	abstract = {We compared the precision of simple random sampling (SimRS) and seven types of stratiﬁed random sampling (StrRS) schemes in estimating regional mean of water-limited yields for two crops (winter wheat and silage maize) that were simulated by fourteen crop models. We found that the precision gains of StrRS varied considerably across stratiﬁcation methods and crop models. Precision gains for compact geographical stratiﬁcation were positive, stable and consistent across crop models. Stratiﬁcation with soil water holding capacity had very high precision gains for twelve models, but resulted in negative gains for two models. Increasing the sample size monotonously decreased the sampling errors for all the sampling schemes. We conclude that compact geographical stratiﬁcation can modestly but consistently improve the precision in estimating regional mean yields. Using the most inﬂuential environmental variable for stratiﬁcation can notably improve the sampling precision, especially when the sensitivity behavior of a crop model is known.},
